//? Title: Sparse Autoencoders: Activity L1 and Mean-Unit KL
//? Subtitle: Batch-coupled penalties, clipping gradients and four matched sigmoid MNIST models
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A reconstruction model can spread information across many active code units. Activation penalties
encourage lower activity while retaining a differentiable encoder. This report separates mean
absolute activity from Bernoulli KL on batch-averaged units, derives their gradients and clipping
conventions, then trains the original four matched 784–64–784 sigmoid models on genuine MNIST.
Plain, L1, KL and combined variants share initialization, batch order and ninety-six Adam updates
each. The KL-only run has the lowest observed test MSE, 0.06077, versus 0.06795 for plain and
0.06780 for the fitting mean. Penalties lower activity but do not reach the target mean 0.05;
mean-unit KL does not itself guarantee sparse codes for individual observations. The results
are a short single-seed comparison, without a downstream classification or compression claim.
)
\toc

> Two different activation penalties

For image rows X, code \(H=\sigma(XW_e+b_e)\in(0,1)^{B\times F}\) and reconstruction
\(\hat X=\sigma(HW_d+b_d)\), define

$$
A=\frac1{BF}\sum_{b,j}|H_{bj}|,\quad q_j=\frac1B\sum_b H_{bj},\quad
c_j=\operatorname{clip}(q_j,\epsilon,1-\epsilon),\\
K=\frac1F\sum_j\left[\rho\log\frac\rho{c_j}+(1-\rho)\log\frac{1-\rho}{1-c_j}\right],\qquad
L=\operatorname{MSE}(\hat X,X)+\alpha A+\beta K.
$$
\alttext(L1 averages absolute activities over observations and units. KL compares the target probability with each clipped batch unit mean, averaging over units. Both weighted penalties add to average reconstruction MSE.)

The target \(\rho=0.05\), clipping \(\epsilon=10^{-5}\) and unit-mean KL follow this small
source experiment. [Andrew Ng's sparse-autoencoder notes](https://web.stanford.edu/class/cs294a/sparseAutoencoder.pdf)
explain the mean-activity KL construction. Here KL is averaged over units rather than summed,
MSE is averaged over pixels, and activation L1 replaces any weight-decay term. Coefficients
therefore cannot be transferred unchanged from differently normalized objectives. Neither
penalty directly penalizes weights or forces exactly zero coordinates.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/sparse_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(54, 1100);
  d.node("x", {0, 0}, "Image rows X\n784 pixels")
      .node("h", {6, 0}, "Sigmoid encoder H\n64 activities per image")
      .node("r", {12, 0}, "Independent sigmoid decoder\n784 reconstructed pixels")
      .node("a", {0, -3}, "Activation L1\nMean absolute activity")
      .node("k", {6, -3}, "Mean-unit KL\nBatch mean → clip → target KL")
      .node("m", {12, -3}, "Pixel MSE\nClean image target")
      .node("l", {6, -6}, "Complete objective\nPixel MSE + weighted L1 + KL")
      .edge("x", "h")
      .edge("h", "r")
      .edge("h", "a")
      .edge("h", "k")
      .edge("r", "m")
      .edge("x", "m")
      .edge("a", "l")
      .edge("k", "l")
      .edge("m", "l")
      .save_svg("build/sparse_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/sparse_method.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The same code contributes to reconstruction and two distinct activity summaries. KL couples observations through unit means; the forward encoder and decoder remain observation-wise.)
\alttext(A sigmoid encoder feeds an independent decoder, an absolute-activity mean and a clipped batch-unit-mean KL branch. All three contributions join the reconstruction objective.)

`models::ActivationSparsity` and `SparseAutoencoder` are canonical C++ components. Populated
handles, including nested penalty results, own their lifetimes and retain gradients. Float32
constants, complements and bounds reproduce the source's rounding; Float64 keeps double
precision. Inputs and weights are checked for finite valid geometry/options before parameter
creation. Tiny signed KL residuals can occur from rounding near its minimum. The core accepts
signed finite codes too; then KL concerns clipped means rather than raw probabilities.
This report's model uses sigmoid codes throughout.

> A four-entry hand calculation, and clipping at the extremes

For code rows [0.1,0.3] and [0.3,0.5], unit means are [0.2,0.4] and mean absolute activity is
0.3. With target 0.2, the first KL term is zero; the second is
\(0.2\log(0.2/0.4)+0.8\log(0.8/0.6)\). Averaging the two terms gives about 0.0457581.
At α=0.1, β=0.2, the first unit's code derivatives are 0.025; the second unit's are
0.025+0.2(−0.2/0.4+0.8/0.6)/4 = 0.0666667. These include both penalties, not reconstruction.

For the source fixture, three rows contain unit means 0, 0.05, 0.6 and 1. The extremes clip
and receive zero KL gradient, whereas L1 still acts on nonzero activities. The program checks
all twelve original Float32 partials and the hand example's four Float64 partials. The curve
uses a larger visible clamp ε=0.01 solely to show the constant tails; training keeps ε=1e-5.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/sparse_penalty.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n              = dm::nn;
  const auto   type        = n::DType::Float64;
  const auto   h           = n::tensor({.1, .3, .3, .5}, {2, 2}, type).requires_grad_();
  const auto   p           = n::models::ActivationSparsity(h, .1, .2, .2);
  const auto   gradient    = n::grad(p.total, {h})[0];
  const double expected_kl = (.2 * std::log(.2 / .4) + .8 * std::log(.8 / .6)) / 2;
  const auto   expected    = n::tensor({.025, 1. / 15, .025, 1. / 15}, {2, 2}, type);
  if (std::abs(p.l1.item() - .3) > 1e-14 || std::abs(p.kl.item() - expected_kl) > 1e-14 ||
      n::max(n::abs(gradient - expected)).item() > 1e-14)
    return 1;
  std::print(
      "Hand L1 {:.8f}, KL {:.8f}, total {:.8f}; derivatives {}.\n\n",
      p.l1.item(),
      p.kl.item(),
      p.total.item(),
      gradient.values_str());
  const std::vector<float> values{0, .05f, .2f, 1, 0, .05f, .6f, 1, 0, .05f, 1, 1};
  const auto               source   = n::from_vector(values, {3, 4}).requires_grad_();
  const auto               fixture  = n::models::ActivationSparsity(source, .1f, .2f);
  const auto               partials = n::grad(fixture.total, {source})[0].to_vector<float>();
  std::print(
      "| source unit | mean | clipped mean | L1+KL derivative per row |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n");
  for (int j = 0; j < 4; ++j) {
    double q = 0;
    for (int b = 0; b < 3; ++b)
      q += values[b * 4 + j] / 3.;
    for (int b = 0; b < 3; ++b) {
      double expected = values[b * 4 + j] == 0 ? 0 : double(.1f) / 12;
      if (q > 1e-5f && q < 1.f - 1e-5f)
        expected += double(.2f) * (-double(.05f) / q + double(1.f - .05f) / (1 - q)) / 12;
      if (std::abs(partials[b * 4 + j] - expected) > 3e-7)
        return 1;
    }
    std::print(
        "| {} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} |\n", j, q, fixture.clipped_mean.at({j}), partials[j]);
  }
  std::vector<double> means, kl;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 100; ++i) {
    means.push_back(i / 100.);
    kl.push_back(
        n::models::ActivationSparsity(n::full({1, 1}, means.back(), type), 0, 1, .05, .01)
            .kl.item());
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(means, kl, "target-to-clipped-mean KL", {37, 99, 235}, 2.)
      .x_label("unit batch mean q")
      .y_label("Bernoulli KL at rho = 0.05")
      .title("Clamping caps divergence and zeros its derivative outside [0.01,0.99]")
      .save_svg("build/sparse_penalty.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Hand L1 0.30000000, KL 0.04575811, total 0.03915162; derivatives [[0.025, 0.0666667], [0.025, 0.0666667]].

| source unit | mean | clipped mean | L1+KL derivative per row |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 0 | 0.00000000 | 0.00001000 | 0.00000000 |
| 1 | 0.05000000 | 0.05000000 | 0.00833333 |
| 2 | 0.60000001 | 0.60000002 | 0.04652778 |
| 3 | 1.00000000 | 0.99998999 | 0.00833333 |
\image(figures/sparse_penalty.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The interior curve is strictly convex; clipping adds flat tails. At either clipping endpoint the implementation passes the incoming derivative, a chosen convention at a nondifferentiable boundary.)
\alttext(A target-to-mean Bernoulli KL curve has its minimum near 0.05 and constant tails below 0.01 and above 0.99.)

For \(\psi(q)=\rho\log(\rho/q)+(1-\rho)\log((1-\rho)/(1-q))\),
\(\psi'(q)=(q-\rho)/(q(1-q))\) and
\(\psi''(q)=\rho/q^2+(1-\rho)/(1-q)^2>0\) in the interior. The minimum is at q=ρ.
Away from zero/clipping kinks, the penalty derivative is

$$
\frac{\partial(\alpha A+\beta K)}{\partial H_{bj}}
=\frac{\alpha\operatorname{sign}(H_{bj})}{BF}
+\frac{\beta\,\mathbf1\{\epsilon\le q_j\le1-\epsilon\}}{BF}
\left(-\frac\rho{c_j}+\frac{1-\rho}{1-c_j}\right).
$$
\alttext(L1 gives each nonzero activity its signed coefficient divided by batch times units. The unit's KL derivative is shared across observations, scaled by batch size, and suppressed outside the clipping interval.)

At H=0 the absolute-value derivative is zero. Clipping at an endpoint uses the inclusive
indicator shown; no differentiability is claimed there. For complete model gradients, set
\(D=2(\hat X-X)\odot\hat X\odot(1-\hat X)/(Bd)\), and let G be the expression above.
Then the encoder delta is \((DW_d^\top+G)\odot H\odot(1-H)\), with the usual transposed
input products and row sums for encoder/decoder weights and biases. The source evaluates both
penalties even for zero coefficients, so no branch silently changes the network.

> Batch means do not specify individual codes

Rows [0.01,0.99] and [0.99,0.01] have the same unit means and L1 as two constant rows [0.5,0.5].
Their KL and L1 penalties therefore agree despite different activity patterns. Also, computing
KL separately on each row gives a larger mean than computing it once on the pooled means,
by convexity when all means lie inside the clipping interval. Global clipping can destroy this
convexity outside the interval, so that inequality is not asserted for arbitrary signed codes.
Gradient magnitudes also depend on the actual minibatch size, including the final eight records.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/sparse_batch.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n         = dm::nn;
  const auto type     = n::DType::Float64;
  const auto variable = n::tensor({.01, .99, .99, .01}, {2, 2}, type);
  const auto pooled   = n::models::ActivationSparsity(variable, .1, .2, .2);
  const auto uniform  = n::models::ActivationSparsity(n::full({2, 2}, .5, type), .1, .2, .2);
  double     separate = 0;
  for (int row = 0; row < 2; ++row)
    separate +=
        n::models::ActivationSparsity(n::take_rows(variable, {row}), .1, .2, .2).kl.item() / 2;
  if (pooled.l1.item() != uniform.l1.item() || pooled.kl.item() != uniform.kl.item() ||
      separate <= pooled.kl.item())
    return 1;
  std::print(
      "| aggregation | L1 | mean-unit KL |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| pooled complementary rows | {:.8f} "
      "| {:.8f} |\n| constant rows | {:.8f} | {:.8f} |\n| KL averaged after splitting rows | "
      "{:.8f} | {:.8f} |\n",
      pooled.l1.item(),
      pooled.kl.item(),
      uniform.l1.item(),
      uniform.kl.item(),
      pooled.l1.item(),
      separate);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(55, 1100);
  d.node("a", {0, 0}, "Complementary rows\n[0.01,0.99] and [0.99,0.01]")
      .node("b", {0, -3}, "Constant rows\n[0.5,0.5] and [0.5,0.5]")
      .node("q", {8, -1.5}, "Same unit means [0.5,0.5]\nSame L1 = 0.5")
      .node("k", {8, -4.5}, "Same KL penalty\nDifferent individual codes")
      .edge("a", "q")
      .edge("b", "q")
      .edge("q", "k")
      .save_svg("build/sparse_batch.svg");
}
```
