\caption(Nine rows in the stated order, using identical columns and display scale. Code controls qualify whether differences depend on the correct observation, not merely on lower mean activity.)
\alttext(Ten digit columns compare the original, four matched models, fitting mean, KL reconstruction error and two frozen KL code controls.)

> Verification and interpretation

The report preserves the original four models, initialization, batch order, complete objectives
and validation rule. Every optimizer update remains visible. Core tests cover all original
fixture partials, double-precision clipping conventions, signed L1 values, batch coupling,
small-model first/second finite differences and invalid options before leaf creation. Python/R
use the same C++ components, including owning nested penalty copies that survive parent collection.
All parameter snapshots, first gradients/batches, full-pool codes/components, and additional
first/final-minibatch penalty probes remain in local archives for independent verification.
Every source array in 41 report archives agrees by dtype, shape and value bytes with the original;
the same 41 source archives agree across normal C++, Python and R workflows. The optional
NumPy oracle (`scripts/check_activation_sparse_autoencoder.py`) verifies 404,800 first parameter
derivatives (maximum difference 1.2e-10), four bit-exact first Adam updates, 10,240 activity-penalty
probe derivatives (maximum difference 2.5e-11), and 5,644,800 pool/control prediction pixels
(maximum difference 1.4e-7), including batch means and loss components. It neither prepares
data nor replaces the demonstrated optimizer loops.

Lower activity is not equivalent to exact per-example sparsity, lower reconstruction error,
classification usefulness or a smaller bitstream. These four short fits demonstrate distinct
regularization tradeoffs under matched conditions; they do not establish any of those broader
claims. Longer fitting, other coefficients and downstream evaluation would be new experiments.

> References

- A. Ng, [Sparse autoencoder](https://web.stanford.edu/class/cs294a/sparseAutoencoder.pdf),
  CS294A lecture notes. Mean-activity KL motivates the penalty; this report uses averaged KL,
  averaged pixel MSE, clipping and activation L1 rather than the notes' full objective.
- Y. LeCun, C. Cortes and C. J. C. Burges, [MNIST handwritten digit database](https://yann.lecun.com/exdb/mnist/).
  The native preparation manifest records source files and official record IDs.
